{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target attributes the nighttime urban heat island primarily to anthropogenic heat emissions, but the problem asks for the single mechanism that best explains the nocturnal gap. Surface thermal storage (daytime heat capture and slow nighttime release from urban materials) is the dominant mechanism identified in urban climatology. The target's own countercondition flags that if anthropogenic heat flux is insufficient, the claim fails; measured anthropogenic heat fluxes are typically a small fraction of the energy stored in urban surfaces during the day and released at night, so relying on anthropogenic heat as the best single mechanism is unsupported.","premise":"Anthropogenic heat flux is generally quantitatively insufficient to be the primary cause of nocturnal UHI; stored heat release dominates.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"a1b2c3d4e5f6","quote":"Anthropogenic heat emissions from transportation, industry, and building HVAC systems directly warm the urban air, and the nocturnal gap is larger in cities with greater population density and energy consumption, as this continuous heat source offsets nighttime radiative cooling."},{"block":"7f8e9d0c1b2a","quote":"If anthropogenic heat flux is quantitatively insufficient to account for the observed temperature differences"}],"target":"4f68c4dabb24956caa9ed2dde70a14b702f76d68e5a2bd3fec814fd88ef7b2c1"}]}